WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:03.649
We're approaching the endgame of Avengers:
Endgame.

00:00:03.649 --> 00:00:07.690
Marvel launches the re-release of the superhero
flick Friday, June 28.

00:00:07.690 --> 00:00:11.460
Though the secretive studio has revealed some
information about the new content fans can

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:15.790
expect to see with the re-release, Marvel
has kept quiet on the never-before-seen footage

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:20.710
fans have been most excited for: a, quote,
"unfinished, deleted scene" that will play

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:22.400
after the credits.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:26.060
Marvel fans everywhere have tossed around
theories left and right about what they might

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:28.589
witness in the re-release post-credits scene.

00:00:28.589 --> 00:00:32.870
But thanks to a recent report from We Got
This Covered, we may now have the answer as

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:34.940
to what the sequence actually is.

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:39.600
Reportedly, the post-credits scene featured
in the Avengers: Endgame re-release is focused

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.660
on Mark Ruffalo's Professor Hulk.

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:45.929
Unnamed sources close to the outlet claim
that the sequence is about a minute long,

00:00:45.929 --> 00:00:49.239
and isn't the type of deleted scene that some
viewers have been anticipating.

00:00:49.239 --> 00:00:53.780
The scene, which Marvel itself has confirmed
is incomplete and was cut from the theatrical

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:57.750
version of Endgame, is said to show Professor
Hulk coming to the rescue when a group of

00:00:57.750 --> 00:00:59.260
children are in danger.

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:04.210
Leaping into action, the big green guy jumps
into a burning building and rescues the youngsters.

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:05.770
Then the scene ends.

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:09.790
Though it sounds like a fun scene, the sequence
is perhaps more notable for what it isn't

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:10.790
than what it is.

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:15.470
It's not, for instance, a tease to set up
events in Marvel's upcoming Phase 4, which

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:17.510
is still shrouded in mystery.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:20.270
It's not about Captain America's trip to the
past.

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:24.780
And it also doesn't reveal any hints about
Hulk's future in the MCU - which, given how

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.520
likable the character is, is fairly disappointing.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:30.020
"Well, we're kinda both like fire."

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:31.910
"But Hulk like real fire.

00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:35.220
Hulk like raging fire.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:38.030
Thor like smouldering fire."

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:42.660
The purported Avengers: Endgame re-release
post-credits scene also isn't the much-discussed

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:46.760
deleted sequence featuring Katherine Langford
as an older Morgan Stark.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:51.820
Langford, whom most know as Hannah Baker on
Netflix's drama series 13 Reasons Why, filmed

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:56.110
a scene in which Morgan Stark appeared in
front of her father, Robert Downey Jr.'s Tony

00:01:56.110 --> 00:01:59.810
Stark after he snaps his fingers using the
Avengers' Infinity Gauntlet.

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:04.540
His health quickly fading after powering the
Gauntlet to defeat Thanos and his army, Tony's

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:08.700
consciousness travels to a "metaphysical way
station" where he sees his daughter, now much

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:12.670
older than she appeared throughout Avengers:
Endgame, who forgives him for making the ultimate

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:15.640
sacrifice and leaving her to grow up without
a father.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:20.140
The sequence is presumably an echo of the
scene from Avengers: Infinity War where Thanos

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:24.400
visits a spirit realm to meet the ghost of
the daughter he murdered, Gamora.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:27.360
"What did it cost?"

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:31.920
"Everything."

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.640
Directors Joe and Anthony Russo clipped the
Morgan Stark scene from Endgame because they

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:42.890
felt it made the already complicated movie
even more complex, but some fans had pinned

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:45.180
their hopes on seeing it in the re-release.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:49.710
If We Got This Covered's report is to be believed,
though, this scene won't ever roll out on

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:50.710
the big screen.

00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:54.240
Still, there's some hope that fans will eventually
get to see that sequence.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:59.960
Avengers: Endgame is set to be released digitally
on July 30, and on Blu-ray on August 13.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:03.760
And it's very likely that the bonus material
for those releases will feature deleted and

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:06.959
extended scenes, hopefully including the Morgan
Stark scene.

00:03:06.959 --> 00:03:11.360
And even if the purported Professor Hulk sequence
isn't the post-credits scene you were hoping

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:15.410
for, there are still plenty of other reasons
to see the Endgame re-release in theaters.

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:19.920
Even though the re-release is not actually
an extended cut, it does reportedly include

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.110
a new introduction from co-director Anthony
Russo, as well as a tribute that's believed

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:28.480
to honor the late Stan Lee, plus a sneak peek
at Spider-Man: Far From Home.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:32.950
Plus, for the first wave of fans, there will
be a limited number of exclusive, free posters

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:36.959
featuring an illustration of the Infinity
Gauntlet with the bittersweet phrase "We love

00:03:36.959 --> 00:03:39.060
you 3,000" stamped along the bottom.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:42.880
Plus, you know, there's the movie itself,
which is still awesome the second time - or

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:44.160
the thirtieth.

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:47.840
Hopefully all that will be enough to make
up for what could be a pretty unremarkable

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:51.230
post-credits scene in the Avengers: Endgame
re-release.

